	"nodes":[
		{"id":"0505208e14794a9f","type":"text","text":"### Novella\n\n- **Definition**: A novella is a work of narrative fiction that's longer and more developed than a short story but shorter and more focused than a full novel — typically centered on a single plot line, a small cast of characters, and one central conflict or theme, without much room for subplots.\n- **Length**: Novellas generally run between 17,500 and 40,000 words (roughly 60–130 pages). Anything shorter usually falls into \"novelette\" or \"short story\" territory; anything longer starts crossing into novel length.\n- **Structure and pacing**: Because of the compressed length, novellas tend to move faster than novels — less world-building, fewer characters, and a tighter timeframe — but they still allow for more depth, character development, and thematic exploration than a short story can.\n- **Examples**: Well-known novellas include _Of Mice and Men_ by John Steinbeck, _The Old Man and the Sea_ by Ernest Hemingway, and _Animal Farm_ by George Orwell — all praised for delivering a complete, emotionally resonant story in a compact form.","x":-445,"y":-137,"width":625,"height":670},
